@meta
id: 12-08
title: Every match, lit up at once
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-slash, search-next
par: 40
minutes: 5
vscode: the find box showing 7 of 7
vimrc: set hlsearch
open: service.config.ts

@brief
Seven service blocks, seven timeouts, all of them called `timeoutMs`. The
payments gateway has started taking up to twenty seconds under load, so
its timeout — and only its timeout — goes from 3000 to 30000.

This lesson is about seeing rather than moving. With match highlighting
on, one search paints every occurrence in the file at once, which turns
"how many are there and which one is mine" from a counting exercise into
a glance.

The other half of it is turning the paint off again. Highlighting that
outstays its welcome makes a file unreadable, and there is a command for
clearing it that does not forget the pattern.

@keys
| `/text` `Enter` | search, and with `hlsearch` on, light up every match |
| `:noh` `Enter` | clear the highlighting, keep the pattern |
| `:set hlsearch` / `:set nohlsearch` | turn the feature itself on and off |
| `$` `i` | end of line, insert before the cursor |

@hints
Two searches, not one. Find the block first, then the setting inside it —
the highlight shows you all seven so you can see which one you are on.
--
`/payments` `Enter` puts you in the right block. `/timeoutMs` `Enter`
from there moves forward to the next one, which is the one you want.
Then it is a single character to insert.
--
On the timeout line, `$` sits on the comma; `i0` inserts the extra zero
just before it. Finish with `:noh` `Enter` so the file is readable again,
then save.

@solution
/payments<Enter>  /timeoutMs<Enter>  $i0<Esc>  :noh<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Two searches used as one coarse move and one fine move: get to the
neighbourhood, then get to the line. That is how most vim navigation
actually looks, and it is why sloppy short patterns are fine.

`hlsearch` is the setting that lights up every match, and it is the
closest thing vim has to the find box's "7 of 7" counter. You will want
it on permanently. What you will not want is the highlight sticking
around for the rest of the afternoon, so learn `:noh` in the same breath.
It clears the highlighting until your next search; it does not clear the
pattern, so `n` still works afterwards.

`:set nohlsearch` is a different thing — that turns the feature off
altogether, and then you have to remember you did. `:noh` is the one you
want ninety-nine times out of a hundred, which is why most people bind it
to a single key. There is a line for exactly that in the starter vimrc
this dojo ships with: Ctrl-L clears the highlight and redraws the screen.

@start service.config.ts
export const serviceConfig = {
  gateway: {
    baseUrl: 'https://gateway.internal',
    timeoutMs: 2000,
    retries: 2,
  },
  orders: {
    baseUrl: 'https://orders.internal',
    timeoutMs: 4000,
    retries: 1,
  },
  customers: {
    baseUrl: 'https://customers.internal',
    timeoutMs: 4000,
    retries: 1,
  },
  payments: {
    baseUrl: 'https://payments.internal',
    timeoutMs: 3000,
    retries: 3,
  },
  payouts: {
    baseUrl: 'https://payouts.internal',
    timeoutMs: 8000,
    retries: 1,
  },
  search: {
    baseUrl: 'https://search.internal',
    timeoutMs: 1500,
    retries: 0,
  },
  email: {
    baseUrl: 'https://email.internal',
    timeoutMs: 5000,
    retries: 2,
  },
};

